          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01XSTG2akJyospsMuwNEF2Ko",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/write-ahead-logging.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)\\ndescription: A durability technique that appends database changes to a sequential log before applying them to data pages, enabling crash recovery without forcing full-page flushes.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Durability\\n  - concept/Database-Architecture\\n  - pattern/WAL\\n  - pattern/Crash-Recovery\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Clear, authoritative explanation of a well-established database pattern; standard implementation approach across major engines.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nWrite-ahead logging (WAL) is a fundamental durability technique used in database engines to ensure that no committed transaction is ever lost, even in the event of a crash.\\n\\n## How It Works\\n\\nIn WAL, every change to the database is appended to a sequential log file **before** the change is applied to the main data pages. This ordering guarantee is crucial:\\n\\n1. Change is written to the log (sequential write, fast)\\n2. Change is acknowledged as committed to the client\\n3. Change is eventually applied to the main data pages (can be batched, asynchronous)\\n\\n## Crash Recovery\\n\\nIf a crash occurs between the log write and the page write, the database can always recover by replaying the log from the last checkpoint. This ensures:\\n\\n- No committed transaction is lost\\n- The database returns to a consistent state\\n- Recovery is deterministic and complete\\n\\n## Tradeoff: Durability vs. Write Amplification\\n\\nWAL introduces a small amount of **write amplification** \\u2014 each change is written twice:\\n\\n1. Once to the log (required for durability)\\n2. Once eventually to the data page (required for data retrieval)\\n\\nThis double-write cost is the price of durability. However, WAL enables two important optimizations:\\n\\n- **Batch writes to pages**: Multiple log entries can be combined into efficient page writes, rather than forcing a full-page flush to disk for every transaction\\n- **Asynchronous page writes**: Pages can be written to disk in the background, decoupled from transaction commit\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n- **Durability**: Committed transactions survive crashes\\n- **Performance**: No need for expensive full-page flushes on every commit\\n- **Recoverability**: Deterministic recovery path from any state\\n\\n## Adoption\\n\\nWrite-ahead logging is used by virtually all modern database engines, including PostgreSQL, MySQL (InnoDB), SQLite, and others. It is a standard pattern in database design.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
